 "answer": "YES -- and by MORE than on the self-play nets. On the same pinned graded exam, against its own matched control, the hybrid saves 1.005 (depth 1) / 1.259 (depth 2) strict moves per shared solve for the supervised pair (44/0 and 52/0 puzzle wins over 197 both-solved, sign p=1.1e-13 and 4.4e-16) against 0.439 / 0.504 for the flagship self-play pair (30/0 and 40/0 over 230 both-solved) and 0.467 for the second self-play pair. It also adds 3 solves (203 vs 200) where the self-play arms added 1. The hybrid is therefore a general improvement to sub-goal planning, not a self-play-specific effect. It does NOT close the gap between the two families: the supervised hybrid at 203/232 and 3.45 mean extra moves stays far behind the self-play CONTROL at 230/232 and 1.42."
